{
  "image_name": "1e7e916cf2c01ed0381506edfe11d700.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes brain circuits that lower or raise body heat production to control hibernation and torpor. In the upper schematic, adenosine acts through hindbrain and hypothalamic regions, including the NTS, PVN, SCN, and especially the VLPO, to suppress thermogenesis. This drop in heat production is linked to entry into hibernation. A contrasting pathway, shown in a different color, promotes thermogenesis and is associated with arousal from hibernation, implying that the balance between these pathways switches the animal into or out of the hypometabolic state.\n\nThe lower schematic focuses on daily torpor and identifies a hypothalamic “Q-neuron” population as a key controller. These neurons are marked by several molecular features, including PACAP, VGlut2, and LepR, and they appear to integrate environmental warmth and fasting signals, with fasting represented as reduced leptin. The Q-neurons send glutamatergic output to the DMH and then influence the RPa through a GABAergic step, ultimately decreasing thermogenesis and producing daily torpor. Overall, the figure presents a coordinated neural network in which metabolic and thermal cues converge on hypothalamic circuits that actively reduce heat generation to drive energy-saving states.",
